00:00:52,014 --> 00:00:53,760
Let's start with something you're probably familiar with.

12
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,000
Now we have the Arduino collection of boards.

13
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:00,160
You can get a variety of different boards for Arduino, and they all have different microcontrollers.

14
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:08,800
So for example, the venerable Uno R3 has an Atmega 328P, whereas the Uno R4 has an Renesus RA4M1.

15
00:01:09,006 --> 00:01:11,428
Now you're probably thinking, okay, Hoff, that's cool, who cares?

16
00:01:11,822 --> 00:01:20,000
Well, actually, it's quite a feat of engineering that all of these boards with their different microcontrollers can use the exact same library reference, right?

17
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:24,000
So you can call digital read on this board, this board, this board, this board.

18
00:01:24,142 --> 00:01:32,960
You can call analog read resolution on all these different boards, you know, and it doesn't matter, you can use the same code on different Arduino boards and it'll just work.

19
00:01:33,454 --> 00:01:39,920
Now, this, my friend, is why you need to know about hardware abstraction layers because it is the wizardry that makes all of this possible.

20
00:01:40,174 --> 00:01:47,280
Now, to bring this to a more professional adjacent context, we're gonna actually dive in and look at how STM32 implements its HAL.

21
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:58,240
For example, if you have used the function HALGPIORIPPIN, you have interacted with ST Microelectronics's hardware abstraction layer that they have made for STM32.

22
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,320
And at first it seems like magic, right?

23
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:03,588
I just call this function and it makes the LED light up.

24
00:02:03,822 --> 00:02:04,960
So how does that actually work?

25
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:06,160
We're going to dive into it.

26
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:15,280
So if you go to the main.c of a modern STM32 project and you do a search for HAL underscore, you will get many, many results.

27
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:21,188
This is standard STM32 initialization code, and you can see HAL is all over the place.

28
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:29,440
And indeed, if you go to your application and you want to do something like blink an LED, you'll be using a function like HALGPIOWritePin.

29
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:32,228
So who is HAL and what does he want from me?

30
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:40,480
Well, if you've got a modern development environment, you can right-click on this function and hit go to definition to get a look at how this function actually works, right?

31
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,680
Because we want to know how the LED actually turns on.

32
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:48,164
When I just do this magic function call, how does it actually turn the LED on?

33
00:02:48,494 --> 00:02:51,280
And at first, it seems a little bit strange, right?

34
00:02:51,566 --> 00:02:58,080
We've got this magic function that's not very many lines of code, and somehow that makes my LED go red.

35
00:02:58,286 --> 00:03:02,720
Now, if we want to unravel the mystery of how this function works, there are a couple of key takeaways.

36
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:08,452
The first is this cast to a Uint32T that's going to this parameter right here.

37
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:13,520
The second is the location in memory that this value is being assigned to.

38
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:18,640
So you can see we've got two memory locations, BSRR and BRR.

39
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:30,500
So the key takeaway from this function is we can manipulate the hardware peripherals on our microcontroller simply by assigning certain values to certain memory locations.

40
00:03:30,958 --> 00:03:34,640
This is MMIO, memory mapped IO.

41
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:40,400
And understanding this is one of the most important things to becoming a professional firmware engineer.

42
00:03:40,622 --> 00:03:44,996
Now, if you haven't seen one of these documents before, I want to introduce you to the reference manual.

43
00:03:45,614 --> 00:03:56,196
Some microcontroller manufacturers will put the information in here, also inside the main data sheet for your microcontroller, but ST Microelectronics likes to split it out into a separate document.

44
00:03:56,878 --> 00:03:58,400
What information am I talking about?

45
00:03:58,638 --> 00:04:01,520
It's the register and memory address definitions.

46
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,560
This is what I was talking about before when we say MMIO.

47
00:04:04,686 --> 00:04:05,700
What is MMIO?

48
00:04:05,934 --> 00:04:14,596
It is nothing but the reservation of a component of your microcontroller's main memory to directly control the hardware of the device.

49
00:04:15,118 --> 00:04:16,160
So let's break it down.

50
00:04:16,366 --> 00:04:28,000
This kind of document looks a little bit scary if you haven't seen this before, but literally all it is is the assigning of memory addresses to certain peripherals in your microcontroller.

51
00:04:28,526 --> 00:04:32,064
So let's say I have an LED wired up to GPIO pin A1.

52
00:04:32,494 --> 00:04:38,864
Well, to manipulate GPIO A, I have to make an assignment to one of these memory addresses in this range.

53
00:04:39,118 --> 00:04:42,304
And if you think about it, that's exactly what we're doing in the HAL.

54
00:04:42,638 --> 00:04:46,916
All the HAL is doing is getting this memory address and making an assignment.

55
00:04:47,086 --> 00:04:54,944
And this reference manual is going to tell us exactly which memory address we need to make the assignment to in order to do what we want.

56
00:04:55,374 --> 00:05:00,804
So if we go one step further here and we hit GPIO register map, you'll see we have all these different register names.

57
00:05:01,134 --> 00:05:04,864
And if we scroll down, one of these is gonna look a little bit familiar.

58
00:05:05,024 --> 00:05:08,064
You can see here GPIO XBSRR.

59
00:05:08,334 --> 00:05:13,344
And that's exactly what our HAL is referencing when we want to turn on and off an LED.

60
00:05:13,582 --> 00:05:21,988
Now if we go to the GPIO section of the reference manual, it'll tell us a little bit more about the registers we can manipulate to get the GPIO to do various things.

61
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:27,236
If we look for our register of interest, we'll find more information about it here, and this is actually quite demystifying.

62
00:05:27,566 --> 00:05:36,464
The bit set reset register is a 32-bit register which allows the application to set and reset each individual bit in the output data register.

63
00:05:36,654 --> 00:05:40,304
Now if we look for what this output data register is, it's described right up here.

64
00:05:40,464 --> 00:05:42,304
It stores the data to be output.

65
00:05:42,384 --> 00:05:46,944
So it stores what that pin is going to output, low or high.

66
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:57,424
So by manipulating this BSRR, we can therefore manipulate ODR, which manipulates the logic level of the PIN, which means does our LED turn on?

67
00:05:57,678 --> 00:05:59,264
Does our LED turn off?

68
00:05:59,534 --> 00:06:05,892
So memory is the key to everything when it comes to manipulating the hardware devices of the STM32.

69
00:06:06,094 --> 00:06:07,184
And this sounds great.

70
00:06:07,344 --> 00:06:08,304
This sounds super easy.

71
00:06:08,464 --> 00:06:12,484
All I have to do to mess with the hardware devices is make a memory assignment.

72
00:06:12,782 --> 00:06:16,420
Here's where things get interesting, and here's where the how comes back into this.

73
00:06:16,750 --> 00:06:18,864
So different STM32 variants.

74
00:06:19,024 --> 00:06:23,264
If you've been paying attention, you'll notice we've been looking at the L432 so far.

75
00:06:23,438 --> 00:06:28,064
But there are a gajillion different STM32 variants with different hardware devices.

76
00:06:28,144 --> 00:06:33,504
You know, some will have a billion timers, others will have multiple SPI or I2C or UART.

77
00:06:33,584 --> 00:06:39,460
You know, some of them will have multiple ADCs or DACs on board, and they all have different amounts of SRAM memory.

78
00:06:39,662 --> 00:06:47,104
So we have all these different STM variants with all different hardware configurations, and therefore all different memory addresses.

79
00:06:47,264 --> 00:06:52,228
You know, each variant will have different memory addresses assigned for different things.

80
00:06:52,718 --> 00:06:55,664
And this is where things get hairy, and this is why we need a HAL.

81
00:06:55,904 --> 00:07:03,908
Now, if you use STM32CUBEMX to generate driver initialization code for you, it'll copy in STM32's HAL library.

82
00:07:04,206 --> 00:07:08,292
And as part of that, you get a file that kind of looks like this.

83
00:07:08,654 --> 00:07:18,308
For the specific microcontroller we've been looking at so far, the L432, there's a huge header file that contains all kinds of different stuff.

84
00:07:18,704 --> 00:07:25,824
But the main thing that it contains for our interesting purposes is a whole bunch of memory address definitions.

85
00:07:26,158 --> 00:07:33,584
So you can see here GPIOA is defined as a GPIO type dev cast of this memory address GPIO base.

86
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:40,836
And if we actually go and have a look at what this means, we can just keep going to the definition here and sort of unraveling the mystery.

87
00:07:41,102 --> 00:07:45,504
This is going to be AHB2 peripher base plus zero, right?

88
00:07:45,584 --> 00:07:48,704
So GPIOA starts right at this base address.

89
00:07:48,814 --> 00:07:54,148
So if we keep going to this definition, we'll find peripheral base plus 0x08.

90
00:07:54,704 --> 00:08:01,000
if we keep going through here, you know, we'll eventually get to peripheral base, which is 0x4000000.

91
00:08:01,904 --> 00:08:07,424
Now, if you do the calculation and you cross-reference that with the reference manual, you'll notice that it starts at this address.

92
00:08:07,504 --> 00:08:14,304
This is the address of GPIOA and it falls under the umbrella of AHB2, which we saw in that header file as well.

93
00:08:14,574 --> 00:08:17,156
Is everything starting to come together and make sense for you guys?

94
00:08:17,710 --> 00:08:33,904
So the upshot of this is that if the register access pattern is the exact same between two microcontrollers, and the only difference is the memory address they need to access, then that means we can just sub in one of these big header files with all the definitions, and it'll just work.

95
00:08:34,064 --> 00:08:35,344
It'll be exactly the same.

96
00:08:35,504 --> 00:08:37,504
The code will be exactly the same.

97
00:08:37,998 --> 00:08:41,664
Now, something interesting to look at here is the names of these files, right?

98
00:08:41,998 --> 00:08:47,172
So if you go up to the top of this one, you'll see stm32L4XX underscore HAL.

99
00:08:47,504 --> 00:08:52,224
That implies that this file will work for any L4 series STM32.

100
00:08:52,494 --> 00:08:56,384
And if you notice, this one is STM32L432.

101
00:08:56,622 --> 00:09:02,724
Now there are plenty of other L4 series microcontrollers, like the L41, the 42, the 44, the 45.

102
00:09:03,086 --> 00:09:11,812
And so that implies that all you have to do is sub in a different header file for your specific variant, and this will work for all of the L4 series.

103
00:09:12,270 --> 00:09:13,860
So okay, Hoff, that's really cool.

104
00:09:14,286 --> 00:09:17,104
But that doesn't answer our question from the beginning of the video.

105
00:09:17,326 --> 00:09:23,984
Now Arduino doesn't just have the same series microcontroller, not even the same manufacturer of microcontroller.

106
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:27,940
Arduino has completely different microcontrollers, okay?

107
00:09:28,224 --> 00:09:30,384
Absolutely just completely different.

108
00:09:30,544 --> 00:09:32,676
I understand how MMIO works now.

109
00:09:32,942 --> 00:09:43,172
I understand that if you have the same register access pattern and you just have memory addresses that change, you know, we can just sub in a big definitions.h file and the memory access will just work.

110
00:09:43,406 --> 00:09:48,624
But what if we have a completely different microcontroller with a completely different register access pattern?

111
00:09:48,846 --> 00:09:50,916
It might not even have the same registers at all.

112
00:09:51,502 --> 00:09:54,864
So what I want you to think of now is a how in a more abstract sense.

113
00:09:54,944 --> 00:10:05,732
We've just looked at the exact implementation of how this works and how it accesses this register in memory and how it manipulates the hardware devices through the reference manual and the memory addresses and MMIO.

114
00:10:05,934 --> 00:10:10,052
But I want you to think of what is a HAL in a more abstract sense.

115
00:10:10,638 --> 00:10:17,504
What it is, is an agreed upon, stable, and reusable API for your application to call.

116
00:10:17,664 --> 00:10:17,984
Okay.

117
00:10:18,414 --> 00:10:26,304
So how GPIO write pin, the contract that this makes with your application when you write this line of code, right?

118
00:10:26,464 --> 00:10:30,244
Or if your AI agent writes this line of code, you know, whatever, you do you.

119
00:10:30,638 --> 00:10:37,104
The contract is that if I give you an abstract definition of a GPIO type def, which is a pointer, right?

120
00:10:37,184 --> 00:10:41,636
It could be a pointer to any GPIO type def as long as I define it correctly.

121
00:10:41,934 --> 00:10:47,104
I give you a GPIO pin in UN 16 and I give you a pin state, right?

122
00:10:47,344 --> 00:10:52,612
Set or reset, that means that you will do this operation that I am intending, right?

123
00:10:52,974 --> 00:10:55,588
The implementation doesn't actually matter.

124
00:10:55,950 --> 00:10:58,544
This could be a completely different function.

125
00:10:58,766 --> 00:11:03,504
Okay, it could access a completely different Maybe my register is called ASRR.

126
00:11:03,584 --> 00:11:05,252
It's not, but like maybe it could be.

127
00:11:05,424 --> 00:11:06,144
It doesn't matter.

128
00:11:06,304 --> 00:11:10,756
As long as it does what I need it to do, the contract is satisfied.

129
00:11:11,118 --> 00:11:14,436
So to illustrate this, we're looking at two Arduino library variants.

130
00:11:14,702 --> 00:11:16,464
This is Arduino Core AVR.

131
00:11:16,558 --> 00:11:24,484
So this is targeting things like the Atmega 328P, and this is Arduino Core SAMD, targeting things like the SAM C21 and the D21.

132
00:11:24,878 --> 00:11:26,464
So I want you to look at DigitalWrite.

133
00:11:26,624 --> 00:11:31,972
This is the Arduino equivalent of the HAL GPIO write pin we've been looking at for STM32.

134
00:11:32,334 --> 00:11:44,064
So you can see that the way the AVR does it, now you don't really have to understand what this is doing, so much so that you should look at the SAM implementation and see that it is actually quite a different implementation.

135
00:11:44,398 --> 00:11:49,824
You know, we do different things, like the code is different, but the contract is exactly the same.

136
00:11:50,094 --> 00:12:02,852
The contract being you give me a pin, you give me a value for that pin, and I will manipulate the hardware to do that for you, even though these two libraries for the two different microcontrollers have a different implementation under the hood.

137
00:12:03,182 --> 00:12:05,424
You still give it a pin, you still give it a val.

138
00:12:05,998 --> 00:12:15,344
And if you're confused by pin size T, by the way, that's essentially just typedeft in here to like UN32 or UN8, depending on the chip that you're using.

139
00:12:15,598 --> 00:12:17,984
So, like at the end of the day, it's still the same contract.

140
00:12:18,126 --> 00:12:23,556
It's like, do you want to give me a pin number and give me a pin value and I will set it for you?

141
00:12:23,664 --> 00:12:30,948
So this is what I'm talking about when I say like even between different microcontroller families, you can have the same contract.

142
00:12:31,310 --> 00:12:36,356
Now to really drive this point home, okay, we have Arduino call Renaissus.

143
00:12:36,782 --> 00:12:41,904
And you can see here it's implementing the digital write contract exactly the same as they were before.

144
00:12:42,158 --> 00:12:46,544
But in this case, we're just gonna do another function call to our IO port pin write.

145
00:12:46,990 --> 00:12:47,652
What is this?

146
00:12:48,046 --> 00:12:50,780
Well, that's actually part of the Renaissance how.

147
00:12:51,024 --> 00:13:01,892
So we have Arduino providing a how layer, and it's calling the Renaissance HAL for the, the, you know, at the register level that we kind of showed you before with the STM32.

148
00:13:02,254 --> 00:13:08,224
So you can see that HALs can have different layers stacked on top of them depending on the level of abstraction that you want to provide.

149
00:13:08,558 --> 00:13:18,404
So you've got the register level house, like I showed you just before, and then Arduino has like entire microcontroller family level house where you've got all this kind of crazy stuff going on.

150
00:13:18,704 --> 00:13:25,956
But that really gives you the flexibility to just be able to provide a single language reference and it'll work on every Arduino board.

151
00:13:26,222 --> 00:13:30,180
So, you know, like you just have to be intelligent with how you use this abstraction.

152
00:13:30,384 --> 00:13:38,308
Another really important thing that the STM32 HAL gives you, for example, is all of these nice typedef handles for your hardware devices.

153
00:13:38,798 --> 00:13:45,024
So for every hardware device you define, you're going to have a handle to that, which is defined as one of these structs right here.

154
00:13:45,390 --> 00:13:50,144
So we've got ADC, we've got DAC, we've got UART, we've got timers, etc. etc.

155
00:13:50,542 --> 00:13:55,824
So to give you a handle, and all of the HAL functions will take in this handle as an argument.

156
00:13:56,142 --> 00:14:03,204
So if I want to transfer data over UART, well, this is the function right here, which takes in one of these UART typedefs.

157
00:14:03,790 --> 00:14:10,148
It's at this point that we realize the HAL is doing a lot more for us than just simply abstracting register and MMIO operations.

158
00:14:10,464 --> 00:14:14,116
It is introducing state management and error handling.

159
00:14:14,286 --> 00:14:20,676
And it can specifically relate those two hardware devices through these typedefs that the HAL introduces.

160
00:14:21,070 --> 00:14:28,932
So at this point, it's more like a philosophical question of where does it stop being a layer and where does it start being a library, right?

161
00:14:29,486 --> 00:14:43,044
But for now, we still call hardware abstraction layer, it's not really that well defined, and you can get away with calling a lot of things a HAL, like just a simple shim layer or a library, you know, it's it's kind of not clear where that lands.

162
00:14:43,534 --> 00:14:45,664
So what does the UART type def actually give us?

163
00:14:45,744 --> 00:14:49,892
Well, the first thing it gives us is an instance which is the UART registers base address.

164
00:14:50,254 --> 00:14:51,664
Now this is really interesting.

165
00:14:51,950 --> 00:15:04,304
What this means is that whenever the HAL needs to actually do one of those MMIO operations we talked about before, it can use this as an instant base address lookup, and it can just add offsets to that depending on what it wants to do.

166
00:15:04,430 --> 00:15:07,184
And we'll notice that as we go through some of the functions later on.

167
00:15:07,374 --> 00:15:16,304
We also have an init struct, which in STM32 HAL land is a set of initial hardware parameters that we want the UAR to operate with.

168
00:15:16,464 --> 00:15:22,384
So for example, the board rate, the word length, the stop bits, the parity, the mode, you know, things like this.

169
00:15:22,638 --> 00:15:29,700
And the STM32 HAL gives you a nice high-level init struct that you can use to specify all of these startup parameters.

170
00:15:29,998 --> 00:15:35,184
And STM32CubMax is actually kind enough to generate this for you automatically.

171
00:15:35,502 --> 00:15:39,108
We've had code gen long before AI in the embedded world, guys.

172
00:15:39,470 --> 00:15:46,564
Now unpacking that, we can come along to the UART setconfig function, which is part of this STM32L4HAL UART file.

173
00:15:46,894 --> 00:16:04,804
And basically what this is going to do is check all of the startup parameters that we've given it and modify the registers accordingly, literally just implementing what is in the reference manual, but in a higher level, nicer way, so that when we're going into main.c, like we don't have to worry about setting the individual registers.

174
00:16:05,070 --> 00:16:11,012
We just define up the parameters we want, and then this HAL is going to take care of the register setting for us.

175
00:16:11,470 --> 00:16:15,972
So we come down in our initialization function, you'll see we're setting a couple of register values.

176
00:16:16,064 --> 00:16:30,372
Now, in generic HAL init functions, basically the pattern that they all take is some register setting values based on the init struct, and then also a couple of switch statements based on the human readable definitions of these things.

177
00:16:30,798 --> 00:16:32,944
And that's basically all it boils down to.

178
00:16:33,134 --> 00:16:40,224
At the end of the day, underneath all of this abstraction, you are still just setting bits in registers to get all of this done.

179
00:16:40,590 --> 00:16:44,420
The HAL just adds nice human readable abstraction layers for you.

180
00:16:44,718 --> 00:16:47,664
Now, if we want to, we can cross reference this stuff with the reference manual.

181
00:16:47,758 --> 00:16:51,504
So let's go to main.c and we'll see what we're actually setting in this init config.

182
00:16:51,918 --> 00:16:53,312
So for example, stop bits one.

183
00:16:53,552 --> 00:16:57,712
Well, I can go to the definition of that, and I can see here it's literally defined as zero.

184
00:16:57,792 --> 00:17:00,672
So for the sake of argument, let's pick a more interesting value.

185
00:17:00,878 --> 00:17:02,244
Let's go with two stop bits.

186
00:17:02,638 --> 00:17:08,352
So if I go to the definition of this, it'll bring me back to this big header file that we explored earlier on in the video.

187
00:17:08,590 --> 00:17:14,832
And it'll basically say that we want 0x2 shifted by 12 positions.

188
00:17:14,992 --> 00:17:17,472
If I hover over it, it says 12 positions, right?

189
00:17:17,678 --> 00:17:22,032
